  "answer": "Excretion is the process by which the body removes waste products and excess substances from the blood. The main organ involved in excretion is the kidney, which filters the blood to form urine. The process of urine formation involves four main stages: glomerular filtration, tubular reabsorption, tubular secretion, and formation of concentrated urine. In glomerular filtration, blood from the renal artery enters the glomerulus through the afferent arteriole. The high pressure in the glomerulus allows waste materials, water, and some useful substances to pass into the Bowman’s capsule, forming primary urine. In tubular reabsorption, essential substances and excess water are reabsorbed back into the blood from the primary urine in the renal tubules. Tubular secretion involves the removal of additional waste materials from the blood into the renal tubules. Finally, the urine is concentrated in the collecting duct with the help of the hormone vasopressin. The concentrated urine is then transported through the ureters to the urinary bladder, where it is stored until it is excreted through the urethra.",
